  "profile_scope": "Whole process includes fixture construction, independent ledger generation/verification, timed HTTP requests and disposal. Relative symbol attribution does not establish query-only CPU or memory ratios.",
  "profile_interpretation": "Four captures have zero lost samples. The global JSON limit helper is absent above the 0.5% report threshold, which does not mean zero cost. Production already trims typed entries before encoding using an ordered per-stream heap. Parsed timestamps appear under that trim and record sorting, while label comparisons and hashing have multiple callers including the independent oracle. Peak heap is unchanged at 66.08 MB; calls differ by 13 and instrumented RSS rises 0.72 MB. No memory advantage is qualified.",
  "next_investigation": {
    "paths": [
      "crates/observability/src/querier/scan/stream_scans/stream_scan_options.rs",
      "crates/observability/src/querier/aggregate/record_matching/sort_loki_stream_values.rs",
      "crates/observability/src/http/response/loki_responses/loki_stream_entry.rs"
    ],
    "hypothesis": "Avoid repeatedly parsing the timestamp string during sorting and pre-encoding trimming while preserving malformed-entry fallback and forward/backward ties; measure actual callers before changing representation."
  },
  "upstream_comparison": {
    "source": "https://github.com/grafana/loki/blob/7a40404f32b3e6464c9cfc6cc7dd75a40f3931da/pkg/iter/entry_iterator.go#L681",
    "approach": "Loki ReadBatch consumes an ordered entry iterator until the limit. Krabka pre-encoding trim already uses a heap of per-stream heads, then still materializes and sorts records. A faster post-JSON helper therefore need not improve these finite-limit HTTP cases. No fresh native upstream run is part of this qualification."
  },
